<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in My Classroom – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Trace simple shapes and letters. Interactive: Draw a symbol or image as a response to something.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace basic shapes and lines to begin representing classroom objects or ideas (e.g., tracing a circle to represent a ball or lines to form a window). Can draw simple symbols to respond to classroom prompts or questions (e.g., drawing a circle when the teacher asks for a ball; drawing a rectangle when asked to show a book). Can create simple drawings of familiar classroom objects (e.g., draw a book, a chair, or a pencil).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "What Color Is It?" chant. The teacher holds up flashcards or realia of classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, crayon). "What color is this pencil?" Students chorally repeat the color (e.g., "Red!"). "What is this?" (showing a book) "Book!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models how to trace a simple line and a circle on the whiteboard. "Look! This is a *red* *pencil*." (Teacher holds a red pencil). "See? Red pencil." The teacher then traces a straight line and a circle on the board using a marker. "Today, we will *trace* lines and shapes, like this. We will *write* by tracing." The teacher pronounces /p/ in "pencil" and /b/ in "book" clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a book?" (Teacher shows a book and waits for "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is this?" (Teacher points to a yellow crayon and waits for "Yellow!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, do we trace or read?" (Teacher gestures tracing and then reading; students respond "Trace!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in My Classroom":</b> The teacher displays a large, colorful poster or picture book depicting a classroom scene with various objects clearly visible and labelled. The teacher points to each object and names it, emphasizing the color. "This is a *red* *book* *on the table*. This is a *blue* *chair*." (Integrating prepositions: "on," "in," "next to").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large classroom scene picture or poster, featuring "pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag" in "red, blue, yellow" colors. Labels for objects. (Authentic picture book with a classroom theme can also be used).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying classroom objects and colors: Responding to "What is this?" by naming familiar items (e.g., "pencil") and "What color is this?" by naming familiar colors (e.g., "red"). Following directions: Pointing to or retrieving items based on instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the *red* *book*." (Students point on the poster).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find the *blue* *crayon*." (Students point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the *chair* *tall* or *short*?" (Teacher points, students indicate).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the *bag*? Is it *under* the desk or *on* the desk?" (Students point).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing simple shapes and lines to represent objects; Drawing symbols as responses. Vocabulary: pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag; red, blue, yellow. Grammar: Yes/No questions ("Is this your pencil?"), Prepositions ("on, under, in, next to"), WH-questions ("What color is it?"). Pronunciation: Initial sounds /t/ in "chair", /f/ in "short".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Trace the Shapes and Lines."</b> Students receive worksheets with large dotted lines (straight, curved, zig-zag) and simple shapes (circles, squares, rectangles). The teacher distributes crayons (red, blue, yellow) and gives instructions: "Pick up your *red* *crayon*." "Trace the *red* circle." "Now, pick up your *blue* *crayon*. Trace the *blue* line." The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance and repeating instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Trace and Match Classroom Objects."</b> Students receive a worksheet with dotted outlines of familiar classroom objects (e.g., a *pencil*, a *book*, a *chair*) next to larger simple shapes that could represent them (e.g., a long line for a pencil, a rectangle for a book). Students are instructed to first trace the dotted outline of the objects and then, using a crayon, draw a line to match each traced object to its corresponding simple shape. "Trace the *pencil*. What color is your pencil? Is it *short* or *tall*? Now, match the pencil to the line." (Emphasizing /f/ in "short", /t/ in "chair").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Interactive Practice: "Draw My Classroom Command."</b> The teacher leads an interactive drawing activity. Students have individual whiteboards or a piece of paper. The teacher gives a command, and students respond by drawing a simple symbol or image. "Draw a *book*!" (Students draw a rectangle). "What color is your book? Draw a *red* book." "Draw a *chair*!" (Students draw a simple chair outline). "Is your chair *on* the desk? No, draw it *under*." This allows for immediate drawing as a response.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task for fluency)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will apply their tracing and drawing skills to create a simple "My Classroom Colors" booklet, representing familiar classroom objects with specified colors.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Classroom Colors Booklet." Each student receives a small booklet of 3-4 blank pages and a set of crayons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "Colors in My Classroom" & Scenario: "Colors in My Classroom":</b> Students will independently draw and color classroom objects they have learned, demonstrating their ability to represent ideas visually and use color vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives clear, sequential instructions for each page of the booklet, modeling on a larger board if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 1: "On this page, draw a *red* *pencil*." (Students draw a simple pencil outline and color it red).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 2: "On this page, draw a *blue* *book*." (Students draw a simple rectangle for a book and color it blue).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 3: "On this page, draw a *yellow* *chair*." (Students draw a simple chair outline and color it yellow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering encouragement and assistance, and checking for understanding (e.g., "Is this your *red* *pencil*?"). Students may say "Thank you" when given crayons or help.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Classroom Colors Booklet" activity and a quick review at the end.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students show their completed "My Classroom Colors Booklet" to the teacher or a peer. The teacher will ask simple questions about their drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can trace basic lines and simple shapes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can draw simple representations of familiar classroom objects (pencil, book, chair).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use specified colors (red, blue, yellow) when drawing/coloring objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to simple "What color is it?" questions about their drawings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows multi-step directions (e.g., "Draw a red pencil").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me your favorite drawing!" (Students point to a page in their booklet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a drawing: "Is your *book* *red* or *blue*?" (Students point to the color or say the color).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection Prompts:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What classroom object did you draw today?" (Students point to their drawing or attempt to say the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like drawing with the *red* *crayon* or the *blue* *crayon*?" (Students point to the color or crayon).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the tracing and drawing activities worked well? Were students engaged and able to follow the instructions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with fine motor skills for tracing or drawing specific objects? How can I provide more scaffolded support for these skills in future lessons (e.g., more pre-traced guides, hand-over-hand assistance)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the target vocabulary words (pencil, book, chair, red, blue, yellow) consistently used and understood in the context of the writing tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will use their drawings and knowledge of classroom objects and colors to engage in a collaborative project, perhaps creating a large classroom collage or arranging their drawn objects based on prepositions (e.g., placing the "blue book on the table," or the "red pencil under the chair").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>